.\" wbindkeys.1 - manual page for the wbindkeys program
.TH WBINDKEYS 1 "September 2026" "wbindkeys 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wbindkeys \- bind keys, mouse buttons and scroll events to commands on Wayland

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wbindkeys
[\-d]
.br
.B wbindkeys permissions
\-\-check | \-\-set
.br
.B wbindkeys
\-h | \-V

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wbindkeys
runs shell commands when key combinations, mouse buttons or scroll events
are pressed. It is a Wayland replacement for
.BR xbindkeys (1),
configured with a Lua script.

Input is read directly from the input devices in \fI/dev/input\fR through
libinput rather than through the compositor, so it works the same under any
Wayland compositor. Because of this, the user running wbindkeys needs read
access to those devices; see
.B PERMISSIONS
below.

Run without a subcommand, wbindkeys loads its configuration and keeps
running, listening for input.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
Print log output to stderr: which input devices were opened (or could not
be opened), the configuration file being loaded, each registered binding and
the key codes it maps to, unrecognised key names, every key press with its
combination, and whether it matched a binding.

.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a summary of options and exit.

.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B permissions \-\-check
Check that the udev rule is installed and that every input device can be
read by the current user. Run it as your normal user, not as root. Lists any
devices that cannot be read and exits with status 1 if anything is wrong.

.TP
.B permissions \-\-set
Install the udev rule in \fI/etc/udev/rules.d/69-wbindkeys.rules\fR, reload
udev rules and re-trigger input devices so the current session gets access.
Must be run as root.

.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a Lua script. Bindings are created with the
.B bind
function, which maps a key combination to a shell command:

.EX
bind("ALT+T", "alacritty")
bind("MOD+Mouse4", "wofi --show drun")
bind("ALT+ScrollUp", "pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ +5%")
.EE

Commands are run with
.BR sh (1)
and their output is discarded.

Key names are case-insensitive and joined with \fB+\fR. The available names
are:

.TP
Modifiers
ALT, CTRL, SHIFT, MOD (the Super key), SPACE, and the right-hand
RIGHTALT, RIGHTCTRL, RIGHTSHIFT, RIGHTMOD. ALT, CTRL, SHIFT and MOD refer to
the left-hand keys.

.TP
Letters, numbers and function keys
A to Z, 0 to 9, F1 to F12.

.TP
Navigation
UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, HOME, END, PAGEUP, PAGEDOWN, INSERT, DELETE.

.TP
Other keys
ESCAPE, ENTER, BACKSPACE, TAB, BACKTICK, COMMA, PERIOD, SLASH, BACKSLASH,
SEMICOLON, QUOTE, DASH, EQUAL, LEFTSQUARE, RIGHTSQUARE.

.TP
Mouse
MOUSE1 to MOUSE10, SCROLLUP, SCROLLDOWN, SCROLLLEFT, SCROLLRIGHT.

.PP
When a binding has several modifiers they must be pressed in the order they
are written. Unknown key names are ignored; run with \fB\-\-debug\fR to see
them.

.SH PERMISSIONS
wbindkeys runs as a normal user and needs read access to \fI/dev/input\fR.
Running
.B sudo wbindkeys permissions \-\-set
once installs a udev rule that tags input devices with \fBuaccess\fR, so
.BR systemd-logind (8)
grants access to the user in the active local session. Check access with
.BR "wbindkeys permissions \-\-check" .

If devices are still not readable, re-run \fBpermissions \-\-set\fR, or log
out and back in. Access is only granted to the session at the physical seat,
so it does not work over SSH.

.SH FILES
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wbindkeys/init.lua\fR
Configuration file. Defaults to \fI~/.config/wbindkeys/init.lua\fR when
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is not set. wbindkeys exits with an error if it does
not exist.

.TP
\fI/etc/udev/rules.d/69-wbindkeys.rules\fR
udev rule installed by \fBpermissions \-\-set\fR.

.TP
\fI~/.config/systemd/user/wbindkeys.service\fR
systemd user service created by \fBmake install\fR.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or \fBpermissions \-\-check\fR found no problems.
.TP
.B 1
\fBpermissions \-\-check\fR found a problem, or \fBpermissions \-\-set\fR
failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line arguments.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Debug a configuration (stop the service first so it doesn't also react):
.EX
systemctl --user stop wbindkeys.service
wbindkeys --debug
.EE

.TP
Grant access to input devices when wbindkeys is in \fI~/.local/bin\fR:
.EX
sudo ~/.local/bin/wbindkeys permissions --set
.EE

.TP
Check access:
.EX
wbindkeys permissions --check
.EE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xbindkeys (1),
.BR libinput (4),
.BR udev (7),
.BR systemd-logind (8)

